### CrowGPT field server — API smoke test
# Run these inline with the VS Code "REST Client" extension (or JetBrains HTTP Client).
# Start the server first:   cd fieldserver && uv run fieldserver     (serves :5100)
# Two secrets, and they are not interchangeable: the enroll token (default
# "dev-enroll", override with FIELD_ENROLL_TOKEN) lets a device join and see its
# own captures; the operator token (default "dev-operator", FIELD_OPERATOR_TOKEN)
# is what /api/fleet/* — everyone's captures — is gated on. Both defaults work on
# loopback only; the server refuses to bind the network with them.
# Requests are ordered; later ones reuse the token/ids captured from earlier responses.

@base = http://127.0.0.1:5100
@enroll = dev-enroll
@operator = dev-operator

### Health
GET {{base}}/api/health

### Config — English (the fleet's command-and-control document)
GET {{base}}/api/config?locale=en

### Config — German (labels/instructions/UI/species all localized)
GET {{base}}/api/config?locale=de

### Config — unchanged since v1 → 304 (client already has it)
GET {{base}}/api/config?since=1

### Negative: enroll with the wrong token → 403
POST {{base}}/api/devices/register
Content-Type: application/json

{ "enroll_token": "wrong", "contributor_name": "nobody" }

### Negative: claim is_operator without the operator token → 403
POST {{base}}/api/devices/register
Content-Type: application/json

{ "enroll_token": "{{enroll}}", "contributor_name": "sneaky", "is_operator": true }

### Enroll this device → returns a device bearer token
# Drop `operator_token` (and `is_operator`) to enroll an ordinary contributor
# device: it can push its own captures but gets 403 from every /api/fleet/ route.
# @name register
POST {{base}}/api/devices/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "enroll_token": "{{enroll}}",
  "contributor_name": "Ben",
  "platform": "android",
  "locale": "en",
  "is_operator": true,
  "operator_token": "{{operator}}"
}

@token = {{register.response.body.$.device_token}}

### Negative: an ingest call with no/invalid token → 401
POST {{base}}/api/captures
Authorization: Bearer not-a-real-token
Content-Type: application/json

{ "latitude": 40.76, "longitude": -111.89 }

### Create a capture (a recording session with GPS)
# `client_uuid` is the device's own id for this capture and makes the create
# idempotent: the app records offline and syncs on demand, so a POST whose
# response was lost gets retried. Replaying the same uuid returns the SAME row
# rather than a duplicate. Optional — omit it and every POST creates a new row.
# @name capture
POST {{base}}/api/captures
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "client_uuid": "b3f1c2d4e5a60718",
  "latitude": 40.76, "longitude": -111.89,
  "duration_sec": 12.5, "notes": "caw near the roof"
}

@captureId = {{capture.response.body.$.id}}

### Replay the identical create → same id, no duplicate (what a retried sync does)
POST {{base}}/api/captures
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "client_uuid": "b3f1c2d4e5a60718",
  "latitude": 40.76, "longitude": -111.89,
  "duration_sec": 12.5, "notes": "caw near the roof"
}

### List my captures
GET {{base}}/api/captures
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Register a media slot for the capture
# Same idempotency: replaying `client_uuid` returns the existing slot, and its
# `bytes` is the offset the next Content-Range should resume from.
# `input_device` is which microphone the phone actually recorded through, read
# back from the audio policy rather than assumed. It is here because it cannot be
# recovered later: nothing in a WAV says it arrived over a Bluetooth headset, and
# such a clip is narrowband and speech-processed — much less use for training.
# `input_honoured` is false when a pinned mic was refused and another was used.
# @name media
POST {{base}}/api/captures/{{captureId}}/media
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "client_uuid": "9a8b7c6d5e4f3021", "kind": "audio", "format": "mp3",
  "sample_rate": 48000, "channels": 1,
  "input_device": "BUILTIN_MIC", "input_honoured": true
}

@mediaId = {{media.response.body.$.id}}

### Upload the audio (whole file, no Content-Range → server finalizes on this PUT).
# Swap the path for any local audio/video file; here we reuse a corvid test clip so
# the analyze step below actually has a real recording to work on once the worker runs.
PUT {{base}}/api/media/{{mediaId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/octet-stream

< ../test_examples/rcrows_sample.mp3

### (Alternative) resumable upload — what the mobile client does over cellular.
# Run these two INSTEAD of the whole-file PUT above, on a fresh media slot.
# PUT {{base}}/api/media/{{mediaId}}
# Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
# Content-Type: application/octet-stream
# Content-Range: bytes 0-9/20
#
# 0123456789
#
# ###
# PUT {{base}}/api/media/{{mediaId}}
# Content-Range: bytes 10-19/20
# ... second chunk ...

### Enqueue analysis (202). Needs the inference worker running to reach "done".
# @name analyze
POST {{base}}/api/captures/{{captureId}}/analyze
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

@jobId = {{analyze.response.body.$.id}}

### Poll the job (queued → running → done, once the worker is built + running)
GET {{base}}/api/jobs/{{jobId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Detected calls for the capture (species + call type + confidence; filled by the worker)
GET {{base}}/api/captures/{{captureId}}/calls
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Free-text observation — the context half, written back at the desk.
# Presets (labels) train the model; this is for debugging a confusing prediction
# and for spotting which situations recur often enough to earn a preset later.
# @name observation
POST {{base}}/api/captures/{{captureId}}/observations
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "body": "Edgar is alone, cawing and searching. I think he's calling the other four home."
}

@observationId = {{observation.response.body.$.id}}

### A dictated note — same field, flagged as transcribed rather than typed
POST {{base}}/api/captures/{{captureId}}/observations
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "body": "Zwei kamen etwa vier Minuten spaeter zurueck.", "source": "voice", "locale": "de" }

### Negative: an empty body is not an observation → 400
POST {{base}}/api/captures/{{captureId}}/observations
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "body": "   " }

### List the capture's observations (oldest first)
GET {{base}}/api/captures/{{captureId}}/observations
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Revise one — interpretation changes as you learn more; updated_at moves
PATCH {{base}}/api/observations/{{observationId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "body": "Edgar is alone, cawing and searching. I think he's calling the other four home. Two returned ~4 min later."
}

### Delete an observation (author or operator only) → 204
DELETE {{base}}/api/observations/{{observationId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### --- operator dashboard (read-only, operator token required) ---
# The dashboard itself is just {{base}}/ in a browser; these are what it calls.

### Fleet summary — the tiles
GET {{base}}/api/fleet/summary
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### All captures across the fleet, newest first
GET {{base}}/api/fleet/captures
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Everything about one capture (media + calls + labels + observations)
GET {{base}}/api/fleet/captures/{{captureId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Contributors and their counts
GET {{base}}/api/fleet/devices
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Label totals — what the retrain corpus actually contains
GET {{base}}/api/fleet/labels
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Stream the stored original (untouched bytes, no transcode)
GET {{base}}/api/media/{{mediaId}}/raw
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
